steps.body:
A **subsequence** keeps some characters of a string, in order, and drops the rest: `one` is a subsequence of `stone`. The longest common subsequence (LCS) of two strings is the longest subsequence they share.

Let `dp[i][j]` be the LCS length of the first `i` characters of `a` and the first `j` of `b`. Compare the last characters of those two prefixes. If they match, that character can end the answer, so add 1 to `dp[i-1][j-1]`. If not, one of them is left out, so keep the better of dropping either, with the `maxInt` helper, since Go 1.16 has no built-in `max`.

```go harness
func maxInt(a, b int) int {
	if a > b {
		return a
	}
	return b
}

{{code}}
```

```go
a, b := "stone", "longest"
n, m := len(a), len(b)
dp := make([][]int, n+1)
for i := range dp {
	dp[i] = make([]int, m+1) // each row needs its own make
}
for i := 1; i <= n; i++ {
	for j := 1; j <= m; j++ {
		if a[i-1] == b[j-1] {
			dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1
		} else {
			dp[i][j] = maxInt(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1])
		}
	}
}
fmt.Println(dp[n][m]) // 3, for "one"
```

A new row starts at zeros, so row 0 and column 0 already hold the answer for the empty prefixes, and the loops start at 1. `a[i-1]` is a byte, which is one character for ASCII text like this. Convert other text to `[]rune` first. Row by row, the cells above, to the left, and up-left are filled before you need them.

There are (n + 1)(m + 1) cells and each takes constant work, so this is O(n * m) time and space.

